// TRIPWIRE MINE casing, UNARMED — the grace phase.
//
// The `tripmine` carrier spawns this where it hit, wearing the impact
// heading (`rotation: "inherit"`), so the body's local +x points INTO the
// wall it stuck to and its lens nub (blueprint x=0) points back out along
// the throw. Everything the mine emits rides that pose: `angle_offset: 180`
// is "out of the wall", `offset` is "further into it".
//
// The grace period is load-bearing, exactly as it is for GADGET. The beam
// shines back down the path the mine was thrown along — which is where the
// thrower is standing. Armed on placement, it would tear the wall out over
// its own owner every time. So this phase emits `tripmine_sight`: the same
// ray, drawn dim, with no worm contact at all. It shows you exactly where
// the tripwire will fall while you still have time to leave it.
//
// It is BOLTED to its wall, not part of it (§10.4 `mount:`). `static: true`
// was the old answer and it was a lie that read well: a static mine is part
// of the world, and the difference only shows when the world moves or stops
// existing. Two bolts through the back face at x = 3 (one px past the art,
// i.e. into whatever it hit) weld the pose exactly as `static` did — and
//
//   * carve the wall out from under it and the bolts have nothing left to
//     hold: the mine falls, still sighting, and goes off wherever it lands;
//   * throw it at something that MOVES — a hoverboard, the THEMIS hull — and
//     the bolts catch the BODY instead of a terrain cell, so the tripwire
//     goes where the vehicle goes.
//
// `reach: 6` is measured, not guessed: a carrier stops at the first blocked
// pixel of its ray, so how deep the casing lands depends on the approach
// angle, and the mount seats the body clear of the surface before bolting.
// Swept over nine approach angles into wall/ceiling/floor, 5 catches both
// bolts everywhere and 4 misses one on a slant; 6 is 5 plus a pixel.
//
// Seating is also what replaced `static`'s other job. A dynamic body spawned
// part-buried used to catch the depenetration ejection (the GADGET-at-the-
// floor failure); the mount backs it out along the way it came first, so it
// is never buried to begin with — measured 0.000 px of drift and 0 of 300
// frames awake on all five surfaces.
//
// `mass: 6` is authored because the derived one is wrong for this object: 9
// metal cells price at 22.5, heavier than a WORM (16), for a gadget you carry
// three of. At 22.5 a mine stuck to a hoverboard sank it 30 px/s; at 6 the
// board holds altitude and the pair sleeps.
{
  "shape": { "blueprint": "bodies/tripmine_casing.png" },
  "material": "core:metal",
  "mass": 6,
  "mount": { "pins": [[3, 1], [3, 3]], "reach": 6 },
  "hp": 200,
  // Dead lens: dull maroon, no light. The armed sibling is the same casing
  // with a live one — the tell is the glow, not the shape.
  "stain": "bodies/tripmine_casing.stain.png",
  "fuse": { "ticks": 90, "variance": 0 },
  "on": {
    // The sighting ray: every tick, out of the wall, cleared past the
    // casing's own half-width (particles always collide with bodies —
    // an un-offset beam would die inside its own emitter).
    "tick": [
      {
        "every": 1,
        "do": [
          { "emit": { "type": "tripmine_sight", "angle_offset": 180, "offset": 3 } }
        ]
      }
    ],
    // Arm in place. `rotation: "inherit"` in a body list = my own angle, so
    // the live mine points exactly where the sight beam has been showing.
    // Remove flushes before the spawn queue: the swap nets to one body.
    "fuse": [
      { "spawn_body": { "body": "tripmine_armed", "rotation": "inherit" } },
      { "sound": "sight_on.ogg" },
      { "remove": {} }
    ]
  },
}
